HVST-cm-1 CAUSE, 0.7.0_AE_CloudOps~SC1, written 2026-10-18T17:2xZ (order AMENDMENT 1, step 3): WHY THE ONE REQUEST SAT from 15:26:59Z (queued by HVST-cl-2's one real invoke) to 17:06:12Z (drained at site Z), about 1 hour 39 minutes.

CAUSE: pull_consumer.py is a poller that a person starts and stops; it is never a persistent service (its own header, lines 1 to 8). It can only run where Bloomberg runs: under PY_AWS with blpapi and a signed-in Terminal. That is the Terminal box at site Z, not dell1. A request queued from dell1 (the pull_enqueue Lambda, HVST-cl-2) therefore drains only when site Z runs the consumer, and nobody had. This is the order's own named cause: "a person-started poller that exists only on the Terminal box at site Z". It is by design, not a fault; the CTO routed the design fork (requests strand by design; delete-on-unhealthy loses requests) to round 99.

EVIDENCE I READ AT STEP 0 (cm1_before.out, 16:46Z to 16:51Z, dell1):
  - queue 1 / 0 / 0 and status REQUESTED, updated_at 15:26:59Z, 190 B (sha256 2587af21f9e5e213d9e42b773e333ebe8a06ec935e7775bcb614167ac07cce88): nothing had touched the request in 1 h 20 min.
  - no pull_consumer.py, model_block_pull, scheduled_daily_pull or bbg_* process and no scheduled task for the consumer on dell1 (207 tasks and 589 processes read with controls found).
  - dell1 cannot run it: the PY_AWS path does not exist here, no interpreter imports blpapi, port 8194 has no listener and no Terminal process runs (row AMGT_HARVST-D1-S64).
EVIDENCE AT STEP 4 (cm1_after.out, 17:16Z): the request drained at 17:06:12Z to 17:06:22Z with status LANDED, after site Z ran the consumer on its Terminal box (site Z's report, relayed by the issuer in msg 8166, MSTR msg 8163: health first, exit 0, 1 message over 1 poll, queue 1/0 to 0/0).

NOT KNOWN TO ME: site Z's own wording of why it sat before it was asked; the relayed report carried the results but no cause sentence, so the cause above is named from my evidence and the order's text. Whether the Terminal was healthy before 17:06Z (the consumer's health read at site Z passed when it ran; I made no health read). It is not a Terminal-signed-out cause on the evidence I have, and not a queue rule (every time I read the queue the message sat visible with count 1: 15:27Z, 15:30Z to 15:39Z during HVST-cl-2, and 16:46Z; I did not read it between, nor after 16:46Z until 17:16Z; no redrive policy, retention 86400 s). One thing of mine in that history: at 15:30:58Z (HVST-cl-2) I made one non-destructive peek (receive-message with visibility timeout 0), which raised the message's receive count to 1; it deleted nothing and hid nothing.
